le_wksp`, `FSE_buildCTable_rle`, `FSE_buildDTable_wksp`, `HUF_buildCTable_wksp`, `HUF_readCTable`, `HUF_readDTableX1/X2_wksp` are all compared byte-for-byte (table *and* workspace), and each library's table is cross-fed to the other library's encoder/decoder.\n- xxhash: streaming with 6 chunk-split strategies (single shot, 1 byte, 4, 16, random, random with interleaved zero-length updates) equals one-shot in both libraries; `copyState` forks mid-stream; `digest` idempotence; seeds 0/1/max; canonical round trips incl. arbitrary canonical bytes; independently-verified reference vectors for `XXH32/64(\"\")` and `(\"abc\")`.\n\n## Rust bugs found: none\nNo divergence between the C and Rust `.so` was found — every mismatch I hit turned out to be either a bad expectation in my own test or a **precondition violation that is UB in the C itself** (asserts are compiled out: the C build has `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE` empty so `DEBUGLEVEL==0`). Those are now documented in the test and the input axes are bounded to the memory-safe/defined domain:\n\n1. `ZSTD_highbit32(0)` — reached by `FSE_optimalTableLog*`/`FSE_normalizeCount`/`HUF_minTableLog` for `srcSize<2`, `maxSymbolValue==0` or `cardinality==0`. Sweeps start at the documented domain.\n2. `HIST_count_parallel_wksp` `memmove`s `(maxSymbolValue+1)*4` bytes out of a 4 KB workspace when `maxSymbolValue > 255`, so `HIST_countFast*`/`count_simple` are only driven with `msv <= 255` (`HIST_count*` clamp internally and are swept up to 65535).\n3. `FSE_BUILD_CTABLE_WORKSPACE_SIZE` rounds **down** (`(msv+2+tableSize)/2`), so `FSE_buildCTable_wksp` `MEM_write64`s ~2 bytes past its own advertised size for odd `maxSymbolValue`. The harness allocates 64 bytes of slack past the *declared* size (which is still what's handed to the library, so error paths are unaffected) and compares the slack too.\n4. `HUF_buildCTable_wksp` has no lower bound on `maxNbBits`: `HUF_setMaxHeight`'s repay loop indexes `rankLast[14]` with `ZSTD_highbit32(totalCost)+1` 